---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="tharavad">
  <h1>tharavad</h1>

  <p>
    <strong>Tharavad</strong> (also spelled Tharavadu) is a Malayalam word for
    ancestral home, joint family, and root lineage: the traditional
    multi-generational house and the extended family that shares it. That is
    the model. Not a product login. A house with keys.
  </p>

  <p>
    We run a public machine so nerds can have a real Linux box: mess around,
    deploy something small, break it on purpose, fix it, repeat. Membership is a
    pull request. After review you get a NixOS microVM under your name, not a
    shared account on someone else’s machine.
  </p>

  <p>
    What you get once you are in:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>ssh you@you.tharavad.xyz</code> from anywhere with a normal key
    </li>
    <li>
      optional public HTTPS: declare a port in git, serve on
      <code>https://you.tharavad.xyz</code>
    </li>
    <li>your own guest; your mess stays yours</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Why bother: a lot of people still wish they had a box at 13 (or later) and
    never get past signup walls, credit cards, or the feeling that the useful
    rooms closed when the forums did. We cannot reverse that. We can keep one
    house open.
  </p>

  <p>
    The site is <code>https://</code> now (Let's Encrypt on the edge). We ran
    cleartext on purpose for a while — see
    <a href="/blog/on-http/">On HTTP</a> — then flipped the padlock back on for
    boring human reasons: browsers scare people, and nobody types
    <code>http://</code> anymore. Rant + apology:
    <a href="/blog/back-to-https/">Back to HTTPS</a>. SSH still uses real keys.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="https://tinkerhub.org">TinkerHub</a> bought the iron. We frob the
    bits, bring guests up, and share the cycles with the community. How to land
    a shell is on <a href="/join">Join</a>. Longer story on
    <a href="/about">About</a>. Notes (rants) on <a href="/blog/">Notes</a>.
    Stay cool.
  </p>
</Base>
